    "result": "`solution.py` now implements `search_products` per the spec, and `tests/test_solution.py` has 54 tests; `python -m pytest -q tests` passes with all 54.\n\nThe implementation uses four fixed SQL statements, one per combination of order column (`name` or `price`) and `descending`, so no SQL text is built from caller input. Name matching uses `instr(lower(name), lower(?))`, which gives a literal, ASCII-only case-insensitive substring match, so `%` and `_` are not wildcards. Ties are broken by `id ASC` in every query. `limit` and `offset` are validated before the query runs, and bools are rejected as integers. The tests cover the stock filter, substring and literal-character matching, non-ASCII case handling, inclusive `max_price`, binary name ordering, tie-breaking in both directions, the limit and offset windows, the exact result keys, read-only behaviour, and a quote-injection attempt. I did not run the coverage, CRAP, mutation or bandit gates locally; they are not part of the command I was allowed to run.",
